---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import GuideNav from './_GuideNav.astro';
import { guides } from './_guides.ts';

const guide = guides.find(item => item.path === 'seo/')!;
---

<Layout title={guide.title} description={guide.description}>
  <main>
    <h1>{guide.title}</h1>
    <p>{guide.description}</p>
    <p>
      Search engines and link previews read the same few tags on every page.
      This starter writes them from one layout, <code>src/layouts/Layout.astro</code>,
      so each page supplies a title and a description and the rest follows from
      your configuration. Nothing here needs a client-side script or an
      external service: everything is plain HTML and files generated at build time.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="titles">
      <h2 id="titles">Titles and descriptions</h2>
      <p>
        Every page passes a <code>title</code> and a <code>description</code>; the
        build fails if either is missing or blank. The layout adds the site name
        from <code>src/config.ts</code> after the page title with a middle dot, so
        a page titled <q>About</q> gets <code>About · Your project</code>. The
        homepage passes <code>home</code> and a short line on what the site does,
        and the site name comes first instead:
        <code>Your project: a short line on what it does</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Search results cut long titles short, so put the words that tell a page
        apart from its neighbours at the start. Write a description of one or two
        sentences that says what the page gives the reader. Search engines often
        build their snippet from the page text instead, so the opening paragraph
        should say the same thing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="canonical">
      <h2 id="canonical">Canonical URLs and trailing slashes</h2>
      <p>
        Each page links to its own absolute URL with
        <code>{'<link rel="canonical">'}</code>, built from the production
        <code>site</code> and the page path. The configuration sets
        <code>trailingSlash: 'always'</code>, so that URL ends in a slash, which is
        the form static hosts serve for Astro's directory output. The sitemap and
        the Open Graph URL use the same form.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pass <code>canonical</code> to point a duplicate, such as a print view, at
        the main page; relative values resolve against your project root and
        fragments are rejected. Pass <code>canonical={'{false}'}</code> to leave
        the link out. During development without a <code>site</code>, the layout
        omits canonical and social URLs rather than guessing an origin.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="indexing">
      <h2 id="indexing">Sitemap, robots.txt and noindex</h2>
      <p>
        The official <code>@astrojs/sitemap</code> integration writes
        <code>sitemap-index.xml</code>. The starter fills it from the pages the
        build actually emitted, and only with pages worth indexing: it leaves out
        pages marked <code>noindex</code>, the 404 page, redirects and pages whose
        canonical URL points elsewhere. You never maintain a route list.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>robots.txt</code> is generated too. It allows crawling and names the
        absolute sitemap URL, or drops that line when no page qualifies. Crawling
        stays allowed on purpose: a crawler has to fetch a page to read its
        <code>noindex</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pass <code>noindex</code> to keep a single page out of search results.
        For a whole preview deployment, build with <code>SITE_NOINDEX=true</code>:
        every page gets the robots meta tag while canonical URLs keep pointing at
        production. The supplied 404 page is <code>noindex</code>, has no canonical
        link and no social image.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="social">
      <h2 id="social">Social images and link previews</h2>
      <p>
        When a page has an image, the layout writes the Open Graph type, URL,
        title, description, site name and image, then
        <code>twitter:card</code> set to <code>summary_large_image</code>. By
        default the build draws a 1200×630 PNG title card for each eligible page,
        so shared links show the page title instead of a blank box. Pass
        <code>image</code> with a <code>src</code> to use your own picture, add
        <code>alt</code> when you want to describe it, or pass
        <code>image={'{false}'}</code> to leave the social tags out. Set
        <code>socialImages: false</code> in <code>src/config.ts</code> to stop
        generating cards across the site.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="structured-data">
      <h2 id="structured-data">Structured data</h2>
      <p>
        The homepage carries <code>WebSite</code> JSON-LD with the site name and
        URL. When <code>src/config.ts</code> names an author, it is the publisher:
        a <code>Person</code> whose <code>@id</code> is the author URL followed by
        <code>#person</code>. If your own site describes you under that
        <code>@id</code>, every project you publish points to one description of
        you. Inner pages carry no structured data by default; add
        <code>Article</code> or <code>BreadcrumbList</code> where your content
        calls for it. FAQ and HowTo markup are not worth adding, because Google no
        longer shows those results for most sites.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="your-part">
      <h2 id="your-part">What is left for you</h2>
      <p>
        Set your production <code>site</code>, your site name and author, and a
        distinct title and description on every page. After the first deployment,
        add the site to Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools, submit the
        sitemap index, and link to the new site from somewhere search engines
        already crawl. Then give each page enough of its own text to be worth
        indexing; no tag makes up for a thin page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideNav current={guide.path} />
  </main>
</Layout>
